1015	With plain strings this ticket scored 1.11 with a confidence of 0.84. With examples it scores 1.09 at 0.87 confidence, a small shift because the plain strings already placed it well. The effect is larger when the plain strings leave the model split, as the next table shows.
1016	
1017	Examples steer the model, and they only help when they look like your real inputs. The table below is the opening Safari report with three different sets of level objects:
1018	
1019	| Level description                                                                                            | `score` | `confidence` |
1020	| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- | ------------ |
1021	| plain string: no object with examples                                                                        | 1.43    | 0.35         |
1022	| Added examples array with useful example: "export fails in one browser but works in another"                 | 1.03    | 0.96         |
1023	| Added examples array with example unrelated to browsers: "search fails, but browsing categories still works" | 1.43    | 0.35         |
1024	
1025	In this comparison, the matching example concentrates almost all the probability on one level. The unrelated example returns the same result as plain strings. Higher confidence does not establish which answer is correct. Choose examples with known expected levels, then test the revised descriptions on separate inputs before keeping them.
